<mxCell id="datan" value="• Reading a secret, uploading a blob, mounting a file share&lt;br&gt;• &lt;b&gt;This&lt;/b&gt; is what the private endpoint, the service firewall and the Palo Alto control&lt;br&gt;• Authorization is separate: Key Vault RBAC, Storage data roles, SAS or shared key" style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;fontSize=19;fontColor=#201F1E;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#8A8886;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="940" y="300" width="810" height="270" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="d1" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;strokeColor=#107C10;strokeWidth=2.5;fontSize=14;fontColor=#107C10;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;fontStyle=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;startArrow=none;startFill=1;exitX=1;exitY=0.3;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;" edge="1" parent="1" source="app" target="kv"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell>
<mxCell id="bottom" value="&lt;b&gt;Two locks on every door&lt;/b&gt;: the network decides &lt;b&gt;where&lt;/b&gt; a request may come from, and identity decides &lt;b&gt;who&lt;/b&gt; may do what. A private endpoint without RBAC still lets any workload behind the firewall read every secret, and strong RBAC on a public endpoint still exposes the service to the internet. You want both." style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;fontSize=21;fontColor=#201F1E;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#8A8886;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="20" y="640" width="1760" height="200" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
</root></mxGraphModel></diagram>
